# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_thread_create(vx_handle task, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_thread_create_id(vx_handle task, vx_handle *out, uint32_t *id);
vx_status vx_thread_start(vx_handle thread, uint64_t entry, uint64_t sp,
                          vx_handle handle, uint64_t arg2);
void vx_thread_exit(void);

vx_status vx_thread_interrupt(vx_handle task, uint64_t thread, vx_str note);

vx_status vx_thread_state(vx_handle task, uint64_t thread, uint32_t op,
                          void *buf, uint64_t size);
vx_status vx_thread_suspend(vx_handle task, uint64_t thread);
vx_status vx_thread_resume(vx_handle task, uint64_t thread);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A thread runs in a task (task(2)), and a task runs while it has a live thread.
Each thread has an id in its task, from 1, in the order the threads were made.
Every call but the first two names a thread by its task's handle and that id; the thread's own handle only starts it, and closing it does not stop the thread.

`vx_thread_create` makes a thread in <task> that has not started, and gives the caller a handle to it with every right.
`vx_thread_create_id` also writes the thread's id to <id>.

`vx_thread_start` starts it, once, at <entry> on the stack whose top is <sp>, 16-aligned, as if `entry(handle, arg2)` were called: on x86_64 with a zero return address just below <sp>, so any C function can be the entry.
<handle>, unless `VX_HANDLE_NONE`, moves from the caller's table to the thread's task, and the thread gets its value there; this is how a new program gets its bootstrap channel.
A thread in the caller's own task gets its handles directly and passes 0.

`vx_thread_exit` ends the calling thread, and never returns.
If it was its task's last live thread, the task ends with the empty exit string, or with the kill's if it was killed.

## Notes

`vx_thread_interrupt` posts <note>, a string of 1 to `VX_ERRMAX` (128) bytes, to the thread <thread> of <task>, or with <thread> 0 to any of its threads not stopped at an exception (ADR-0010).
A call the thread is blocked in returns `VX_ERR_INTERRUPTED`, and on its way back to user mode the thread is diverted to the task's in-task handler (exception(2)) with an exception of kind `VX_EXCEPTION_INTERRUPT` holding the note.
Up to eight notes wait for each thread, delivered oldest first, each its own exception; a handler may be entered again before the last has resumed, as POSIX's do.

A task with no in-task handler ends instead, with the note as its exit string, as a Plan 9 process that has not called `notify` does.
If the handler has gone, or the thread's stack cannot take the exception, when the note is delivered, the note ends the task the same way.

## Stopping a thread

`vx_thread_suspend` stops <thread> before it next returns to user mode, and returns once it has: parked there, or blocked in a call, which will park it on the way out.
With <thread> 0 it stops every thread of the task, as a process is stopped whole.
Suspensions are counted: `vx_thread_resume` takes one away, and the thread goes on when none is left.
A thread that suspends itself returns from the call and parks on its way out.
Stopping a process is this, not a note (ADR-0011).

## Registers

`vx_thread_state` reads or changes a thread's state, by <op>, through <buf>, which holds at least <size> bytes:

: `VX_STATE_GET_EXCEPTION`
  The `vx_exception` a thread stopped at an exception port stopped at (exception(2)).
: `VX_STATE_GET_REGS`, `VX_STATE_SET_REGS`
  Its user registers, a `vx_regs`.
: `VX_STATE_GET_FPREGS`, `VX_STATE_SET_FPREGS`
  The legacy part of its FP/SIMD registers, a `vx_fpregs`: x86_64's FXSAVE image (x87, MXCSR, XMM0 to XMM15), aarch64's V0 to V31 with FPCR and FPSR.
  A SET leaves the rest of the state as it was.
: `VX_STATE_GET_XSTATE`, `VX_STATE_SET_XSTATE`
  All of its FP/SIMD state, `vx_cpu_info.xstate_size` bytes: on x86_64 XSAVE's standard image of every component the kernel saves (x87, SSE, AVX, and AVX-512's three where the CPU has them), every component written out, those in their initial state included; on aarch64 the `vx_fpregs` image.
  A SET whose XSAVE header names a component the kernel does not save, has reserved bytes set or a compacted format, or whose MXCSR has a bit `mxcsr_mask` lacks, is refused with `VX_ERR_INVALID`, as is an aarch64 FPCR or FPSR with undefined bits (ADR-0035).
: `VX_STATE_GET_CPU`
  With <thread> 0, a `vx_cpu_info`: `xstate_size`, `keys` (protection keys a task may allocate; 0 until M6 step 6c4), and on x86_64 `xfeatures` (XCR0) and `mxcsr_mask`, on aarch64 the ID registers user code needs to choose code paths (`ID_AA64ISAR0_EL1` to `ISAR2`, `PFR0`, `PFR1`, `ZFR0`, `SMFR0`, `MMFR3`), the fields of what the kernel does not save, SVE and SME, zeroed.
  User code on x86_64 asks CPUID for instruction sets; aarch64's ID registers trap at EL0.
: `VX_STATE_GET_TLS`, `VX_STATE_SET_TLS`
  Its thread pointer, a `uint64_t`: x86_64's FS base, aarch64's `TPIDR_EL0`.
  With <thread> 0, the caller's own, at any time (musl's `__set_thread_area`).
: `VX_STATE_NEXT_THREAD`
  At any time, the live thread with the next id after <thread> (0 for the first) in a `vx_thread_info`: its id, whether it is running, blocked, stopped at an exception port or suspended, its suspend count, and whether it stopped at a debugger's port.
  This is how procfs(4) lists a process's threads.
: `VX_STATE_GET_WATCH`, `VX_STATE_SET_WATCH`
  With <thread> 0, the task's watchpoints, a `vx_watches`, which all its threads take from when each next runs.
  Each slot is off, or watches writes or reads and writes at an address aligned to its length of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes; GET says in `count` how many the hardware has (x86_64 four, aarch64 two to sixteen).
  A watched access raises `VX_EXCEPTION_WATCHPOINT`.

The register operations work on a thread stopped at an exception port, or, for a holder of `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG`, a suspended one that holds still; a running thread is neither read nor changed.
Registers set are checked: a thread may be given any user-mode state and never a privileged one, so a program counter or stack pointer outside user space is refused, and flags a user may not set are dropped.
FP/SIMD registers set with `SET_FPREGS` are made safe to load the same way; `SET_XSTATE` refuses what it cannot load instead.

## Threads in vx-rt

A native program makes its threads with vx-rt rather than these calls directly (M6 step 6d1):

```c
vx_status vx_thread_spawn(vx_thread *t, void (*fn)(void *), void *arg,
                          uint64_t stack_size);
void vx_thread_join(vx_thread *t);
bool vx_thread_stack(uint64_t *lo, uint64_t *hi);
void vx_mutex_lock(vx_mutex *m);
void vx_mutex_unlock(vx_mutex *m);
```

`vx_thread_spawn` runs <fn>(<arg>) on a new thread of the program's own task, with a stack of <stack_size> bytes (0 for 256 KiB) and its own copy of the program's `thread_local` storage: the image's `PT_TLS` segment, its initialised part copied and the rest zeroed, at its alignment, with the thread pointer set as the ELF ABI has it before <fn> runs.
The stack, the storage and vx-rt's record of the thread are one VMO, below which the unmapped page `as_map` leaves is the stack's guard.
`vx_thread_join` waits for <fn> to return, then lets the thread's memory go.
`vx_thread_stack` gives the calling thread's stack, [<lo>, <hi>), the first thread's included (the mapping its stack pointer is in); false on a thread made with `vx_thread_create` alone, which has no record.
The first thread's `thread_local` storage is set up at start-up, before `vx_main`.
`vx_mutex` is a lock on a futex word, not recursive; vx-rt's printing holds one, so lines from different threads do not mix.

## Rights

`vx_thread_create` needs `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE` on <task>, and `vx_thread_start` `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE` on <thread> and `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER` on <handle>.
`vx_thread_interrupt` and `vx_thread_state` need `VX_RIGHT_MANAGE` on <task>, except the caller's own thread pointer, which needs only a handle to its own task, and the watchpoints, which need `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG` alone.
`vx_thread_suspend` and `vx_thread_resume` need `VX_RIGHT_DEBUG`.

# EXAMPLES

A second thread in the caller's own task, from `tests/kernel/ktest.c`; a thread starts only once:

```c
CHECK(vx_thread_create(self, &th) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_thread_start(th, (uint64_t)worker, sp, 0, (uint64_t)&s) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_thread_start(th, (uint64_t)worker, sp, 0, (uint64_t)&s) ==
      VX_ERR_BAD_STATE);
```

