# DIAGNOSTICS

Every call returns `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE` for a handle that is not open or of the wrong type, and `VX_ERR_ACCESS` for one without the right it needs.

`vx_thread_create` and `vx_thread_create_id` return:

: `VX_OK`
  The handle is in <out>.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE`
  <task> is ending or has ended.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  No room for the thread or its kernel stack.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <out> or <id> is not mapped writable.

`vx_thread_start` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  The thread runs.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <entry> or <sp> is outside user space.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE`
  The thread has started already, or its task is ending, has ended, or is in an exec.
: `VX_ERR_NO_MEMORY`
  The task's handle table is full.

`vx_thread_interrupt` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  The note is queued, or, with no handler, the task is ending with it.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <note> is empty, longer than `VX_ERRMAX`, or not mapped.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE`
  The task is ending or has ended.
: `VX_ERR_NOT_FOUND`
  No thread <thread>, or with 0, every thread is stopped at an exception.
: `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`
  Eight notes already wait for the thread; the caller may try again.

`vx_thread_state` returns:

: `VX_OK`
  Done.
: `VX_ERR_INVALID`
  <op> is unknown; <buf> is not mapped; a register set is refused; a watchpoint is malformed or beyond the hardware's count; a watch operation names a thread; or a thread pointer of thread 0 is asked of another task.
: `VX_ERR_TOO_SMALL`
  <size> is smaller than what <op> reads or writes.
: `VX_ERR_RANGE`
  A thread pointer outside user space.
: `VX_ERR_NOT_FOUND`
  No live thread <thread>, or none after it.
: `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE`
  The thread is running: neither stopped at an exception port nor, for a debugger, suspended and still; or `VX_STATE_GET_EXCEPTION` of a thread that is only suspended.

`vx_thread_suspend` and `vx_thread_resume` return `VX_OK`; `VX_ERR_NOT_FOUND` when the task has no thread <thread>, or with 0 none at all; and with 0, the first failure among its threads, the rest still done.
`vx_thread_suspend` returns `VX_ERR_TIMED_OUT` when the thread has not stopped within a second; it stays counted, and `vx_thread_resume` undoes it.
`vx_thread_resume` returns `VX_ERR_BAD_STATE` for a thread that is not suspended.

# BUGS

When `vx_thread_start` fails after taking <handle>, the handle is closed in the thread's task: the caller has lost it.
<handle> is taken before <entry> and <sp> are checked, so even a bad address loses it.

A 9P connection is one thread's at a time: two threads of a program reading files through vx-ns at once may mix their replies, until 6d4's pipelined client.

The design queues notes per task (ADR-0010); the kernel queues them per thread, so a note to thread 0 goes to one thread chosen when it is posted.
